# Blockchain Table Access Method

{info:title=Core Storage Engine Implementation}
The Blockchain Table Access Method (TAM) is the heart of the PostgreSQL Blockchain Extension, implementing PostgreSQL's Table Access Method interface with blockchain-specific extensions for immutable storage.
{info}

{toc:printable=true|style=disc|maxLevel=3}

## Overview

### What is a Table Access Method?

{panel:title=PostgreSQL TAM Interface|borderColor=#2196F3|titleBGColor=#E3F2FD}
A Table Access Method in PostgreSQL is a pluggable storage engine interface that defines how data is stored, retrieved, and managed at the storage layer. The blockchain TAM implements this interface to provide:

* **Storage operations**: INSERT, SELECT (no UPDATE/DELETE)
* **Tuple management**: Row creation, access, and metadata handling
* **Transaction integration**: ACID compliance and WAL logging
* **Query optimization**: Integration with PostgreSQL's planner
{panel}

### Blockchain TAM Architecture

{code:title=TAM Component Structure}
PostgreSQL Query Engine
    ↓
Table Access Method Interface
    ↓
Blockchain TAM Implementation
├── Tuple Operations ← INSERT/SELECT handling
├── System Column Manager ← 9 hidden columns
├── Hash Chain Integration ← SHA-256 linking
├── Counter Integration ← Global sequencing
└── Immutability Enforcement ← Operation blocking
    ↓
Storage Layer (Heap)
{code}

## Implementation Details

### Core TAM Functions

The blockchain TAM implements all required TAM interface functions:

{expand:title=Essential TAM Function Implementation}
{code:c|title=Key TAM Interface Functions}
// Main TAM routine structure
static const TableAmRoutine blockchain_am_methods = {
    .type = T_TableAmRoutine,
    
    // Scan operations
    .scan_begin = blockchain_scan_begin,
    .scan_end = blockchain_scan_end,
    .scan_rescan = blockchain_scan_rescan,
    .scan_getnextslot = blockchain_scan_getnextslot,
    
    // Tuple operations
    .tuple_insert = blockchain_tuple_insert,        // Only modification allowed
    .tuple_insert_speculative = NULL,               // Not supported
    .tuple_complete_speculative = NULL,             // Not supported
    .tuple_update = NULL,                           // Blocked for immutability
    .tuple_delete = NULL,                           // Blocked for immutability
    .tuple_lock = blockchain_tuple_lock,
    
    // Relation operations
    .relation_size = blockchain_relation_size,
    .relation_needs_toast_table = blockchain_relation_needs_toast_table,
    
    // Index operations
    .index_build_range_scan = NULL,                 // No indexes supported
    .index_validate_scan = NULL,                    // No indexes supported
};
{code}
{expand}

### System Column Management

{section}
{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=9 System Columns|borderColor=#4CAF50|titleBGColor=#E8F5E8}
Every blockchain table automatically includes these columns:

| Column | Type | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `__row_id` | UUID | Unique row identifier |
| `__curr_hash` | BYTEA | Current row hash |
| `__prev_hash` | BYTEA | Previous row hash |
| `__tx_type` | TEXT | Transaction type |
| `__tx_lsn` | BIGINT | Counter value |
| `__tx_origin` | UUID | Transaction origin |
| `__tx_version` | INTEGER | Row version |
| `__is_latest` | BOOLEAN | Latest flag |
| `__tx_timestamp` | TIMESTAMPTZ | Timestamp |
{panel}
{column}

{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Column Properties|borderColor=#FF9800|titleBGColor=#FFF3E0}
**Automatic Management:**
* Populated during INSERT operations
* Hidden from `SELECT *` queries
* Accessible when explicitly named
* Protected from user modification

**Storage:**
* ~104 bytes additional storage per row
* Optimized for minimal overhead
* Integrated with PostgreSQL's storage
{panel}
{column}
{section}

### Insert Operation Flow

{expand:title=Detailed Insert Operation Implementation}
{code:c|title=blockchain_tuple_insert Implementation}
static void
blockchain_tuple_insert(Relation relation, TupleTableSlot *slot,
                        CommandId cid, int options, BulkInsertState bistate)
{
    // 1. Validate this is a blockchain table
    if (relation->rd_rel->relkind != RELKIND_BLOCKCHAIN_TABLE)
        ereport(ERROR, (errmsg("not a blockchain table")));
    
    // 2. Get next counter value
    uint64 counter = BlockchainGetNextCounter(RelationGetRelid(relation));
    
    // 3. Compute previous hash (from last row)
    bytea *prev_hash = get_previous_hash(relation);
    
    // 4. Populate system columns
    populate_blockchain_system_columns(slot, counter, prev_hash);
    
    // 5. Compute current hash including all data
    bytea *curr_hash = compute_curr_hash_with_counter(
        relation, slot, GetCurrentTimestamp(), prev_hash, counter);
    
    // 6. Update current hash in slot
    update_current_hash_in_slot(slot, curr_hash);
    
    // 7. Store the tuple using heap storage
    heap_insert(relation, slot, cid, options, bistate);
    
    // 8. Log for WAL (transaction durability)
    if (RelationNeedsWAL(relation))
        log_blockchain_insert(relation, slot);
}
{code}
{expand}

### System Column Population

{expand:title=System Column Population Logic}
{code:c|title=populate_blockchain_system_columns Function}
static void
populate_blockchain_system_columns(TupleTableSlot *slot, uint64 counter, bytea *prev_hash)
{
    // Generate unique row ID
    slot->__row_id = generate_uuid();
    
    // Set transaction metadata
    slot->__tx_type = "INSERT";
    slot->__tx_lsn = counter;
    slot->__tx_origin = get_transaction_origin_uuid();
    slot->__tx_version = 1;
    slot->__is_latest = true;
    slot->__tx_timestamp = GetCurrentTimestamp();
    
    // Set hash chain linkage
    slot->__prev_hash = prev_hash;
    // __curr_hash will be computed after all other columns are set
}
{code}
{expand}

## Query Processing Integration

### SELECT Operations

{panel:title=Query Processing Flow|borderColor=#673AB7|titleBGColor=#EDE7F6}
{code:title=SELECT Query Processing}
1. Query Parser → Blockchain TAM: SELECT * FROM blockchain_table
2. TAM → Column Filter: Hide system columns for SELECT *
3. TAM → Heap Storage: Sequential scan of tuples
4. Heap Storage → TAM: Raw tuple data
5. TAM → Query Engine: Filtered tuple (user columns only)
6. Query Engine → Client: Result set
{code}
{panel}

### System Column Access

{expand:title=System Column Visibility Control}
{code:c|title=Column Visibility Implementation}
// Hide system columns from SELECT * queries
static bool
should_hide_column_from_select_star(const char *colname)
{
    // All blockchain system columns start with __
    if (strncmp(colname, "__", 2) == 0)
        return true;
    
    return false;
}

// Allow explicit access to system columns
static TupleTableSlot *
blockchain_scan_getnextslot(TableScanDesc scan, ScanDirection direction, TupleTableSlot *slot)
{
    // Get next tuple from heap
    if (!heap_getnextslot(scan, direction, slot))
        return NULL;
    
    // Apply column filtering based on query context
    if (scan->rs_flags & SO_HIDE_SYSTEM_COLUMNS)
        filter_system_columns(slot);
    
    return slot;
}
{code}
{expand}

## Storage Integration

### Heap Storage Compatibility

{section}
{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Storage Layer Integration|borderColor=#795548|titleBGColor=#EFEBE9}
The blockchain TAM leverages PostgreSQL's existing heap storage:

**Benefits:**
* Full ACID transaction support
* Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) integration
* Buffer management optimization
* Backup and recovery compatibility
{panel}
{column}

{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Storage Characteristics|borderColor=#607D8B|titleBGColor=#ECEFF1}
**Storage Behavior:**
* Uses standard PostgreSQL pages
* System columns stored inline
* Hash values stored as bytea
* Compatible with existing tools
{panel}
{column}
{section}

### Memory Management

{expand:title=Memory Management Strategy}
{code:c|title=Memory Context Usage}
// Use appropriate memory contexts for different operations
static MemoryContext blockchain_context = NULL;

static void
ensure_blockchain_memory_context(void)
{
    if (blockchain_context == NULL)
    {
        blockchain_context = AllocSetContextCreate(
            TopMemoryContext,
            "Blockchain TAM Context",
            ALLOCSET_DEFAULT_SIZES);
    }
}

// Hash computation uses temporary memory
static bytea *
compute_hash_in_temp_context(Relation rel, TupleTableSlot *slot)
{
    MemoryContext oldcontext = MemoryContextSwitchTo(blockchain_context);
    
    bytea *hash = compute_sha256_hash(rel, slot);
    
    MemoryContextSwitchTo(oldcontext);
    return hash;
}
{code}
{expand}

## Performance Optimizations

### Scan Performance

{panel:title=Sequential Scan Optimizations|borderColor=#4CAF50|titleBGColor=#E8F5E8}
**Optimization Strategies:**

* **Prefetching**: Efficient page prefetching for sequential scans
* **Column Filtering**: Early filtering of system columns when not needed
* **Memory Management**: Optimized memory allocation patterns
* **Buffer Usage**: Efficient use of PostgreSQL's buffer pool
{panel}

### Insert Performance

{section}
{column:width=50%}
| Component | Time | Optimization |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Counter Increment** | <1µs | Cached in shared memory |
| **Hash Computation** | ~2µs | Optimized SHA-256 implementation |
| **System Column Population** | ~1µs | Minimal data copying |
| **Heap Insert** | ~100µs | Standard PostgreSQL performance |
{column}

{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Total Overhead|borderColor=#FF9800|titleBGColor=#FFF3E0}
**~5% overhead** compared to regular tables

This includes all blockchain-specific operations:
* Hash chain computation
* Counter management
* System column handling
{panel}
{column}
{section}

## Error Handling

### Immutability Enforcement

{expand:title=Operation Blocking Implementation}
{code:c|title=Blocked Operations}
// UPDATE operations are blocked at TAM level
static void
blockchain_tuple_update(Relation relation, ItemPointer otid, TupleTableSlot *slot,
                       CommandId cid, Snapshot snapshot, Snapshot crosscheck,
                       bool wait, TM_Result *tmresult, TM_FailureData *tmfd)
{
    ereport(ERROR,
            (errcode(ERRCODE_FEATURE_NOT_SUPPORTED),
             errmsg("UPDATE is not supported on blockchain tables"),
             errdetail("Blockchain tables are immutable"),
             errhint("Use INSERT to add new versions of data")));
}

// DELETE operations are blocked at TAM level
static TM_Result
blockchain_tuple_delete(Relation relation, ItemPointer tid, CommandId cid,
                       Snapshot snapshot, Snapshot crosscheck, bool wait,
                       TM_FailureData *tmfd, bool changingPart)
{
    ereport(ERROR,
            (errcode(ERRCODE_FEATURE_NOT_SUPPORTED),
             errmsg("DELETE is not supported on blockchain tables"),
             errdetail("Blockchain tables are immutable")));
}
{code}
{expand}

### Error Codes and Messages

{panel:title=Blockchain TAM Error Handling|borderColor=#F44336|titleBGColor=#FFEBEE}
| Error Code | Situation | Message |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ERRCODE_FEATURE_NOT_SUPPORTED` | UPDATE/DELETE attempted | Operation not supported on blockchain tables |
| `ERRCODE_WRONG_OBJECT_TYPE` | TAM called on wrong table type | Not a blockchain table |
| `ERRCODE_DATA_CORRUPTED` | Hash chain validation failure | Hash chain integrity violation |
| `ERRCODE_INTERNAL_ERROR` | Counter system failure | Counter management error |
{panel}

## Integration Points

### PostgreSQL Subsystem Integration

{expand:title=Deep PostgreSQL Integration}
**Query Planner Integration:**
* Custom cost estimation for blockchain operations
* Optimization hints for sequential scan patterns
* Statistics collection for blockchain tables

**Transaction System Integration:**
* Full ACID compliance maintained
* Proper isolation level handling
* Deadlock detection compatibility

**WAL Integration:**
* Blockchain operations are WAL-logged
* Recovery procedures handle blockchain data
* Replication compatibility (with considerations)

**Catalog Integration:**
* Blockchain table metadata in system catalogs
* Custom relation kind (`RELKIND_BLOCKCHAIN_TABLE`)
* System column definitions in catalogs
{expand}

### Extension Interface

{section}
{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=TAM Registration|borderColor=#3F51B5|titleBGColor=#E8EAF6}
{code:c|title=TAM Registration}
// Register the blockchain TAM
Datum
blockchain_am_handler(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS)
{
    PG_RETURN_POINTER(&blockchain_am_methods);
}

// Extension initialization
void
_PG_init(void)
{
    register_blockchain_tam();
    initialize_counter_system();
    setup_utility_hooks();
}
{code}
{panel}
{column}

{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=SQL Interface|borderColor=#9C27B0|titleBGColor=#F3E5F5}
{code:sql|title=SQL TAM Usage}
-- Create table using blockchain TAM
CREATE BLOCKCHAIN TABLE audit_log (...);

-- Internally translates to:
CREATE TABLE audit_log (...) 
USING blockchain_access_method;
{code}
{panel}
{column}
{section}

## Testing and Validation

### TAM Function Testing

{expand:title=Comprehensive TAM Testing}
{code:sql|title=TAM Validation Tests}
-- Test basic insert functionality
INSERT INTO test_blockchain_table (data) VALUES ('test');

-- Test system column access
SELECT data, __tx_lsn, __curr_hash FROM test_blockchain_table;

-- Test immutability enforcement
-- These should all fail:
UPDATE test_blockchain_table SET data = 'modified';
DELETE FROM test_blockchain_table;
TRUNCATE test_blockchain_table;

-- Test scan operations
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM test_blockchain_table;
SELECT * FROM test_blockchain_table WHERE data LIKE 'test%';
{code}
{expand}

